{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 -c \\\"\nimport torch, time, math\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\n# Let me think about a totally different approach.\n# What if we compute in the original x-space instead of v-space?\n# In v-space, we project x->v and then do p@v.\n# In x-space, we do p@x and then project through wv.\n# \n# Cost comparison for the 'compute' part (after p_mix is computed):\n# V-space: p_mix @ v = (B,H,L,L) @ (B,H,L,D) -> (B,H,L,D), cost: B*H*L*L*D\n# X-space: p_mix @ x = (B,H,L,L) @ (B,1,L,C) -> (B,H,L,C), cost: B*H*L*L*C\n# Then final projection: mixed*ratio through wv = einsum('bhlc,hdc->bhld', ...), cost: B*L*H*D*C\n#\n# For L=256, C=1280, H=20, D=64:\n# V-space p@v: 1*20*256*256*64 = 83.9M\n# X-space p@x: 1*20*256*256*1280 = 1.68B  <-- much worse\"'!\n# \n# So v-space is much better for the p@v part.\n#\n# BUT: the Gram approach costs 2*B*H*L*L*L = 2*1*20*256''^3 = 671M for L=256\n# The p@x norm approach costs B*H*L*L*C = 1.68B\n# Gram is better.\n#\n# What if we compute norm differently?\n# ||p_mix @ v||? No wait, we need ||p_mix @ x|| not ||p_mix @ v||.\n# Actually wait, re-read the problem: r uses ||u1|| and ||m|| where u0=P0 x, u1=P1 x, m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0)\n# These are in C-space, not D-space.\n#\n# Alternative: can we compute ||u|| from q,k projections? No, not easily.\n#\n# What about computing: \n# u1 = p1 @ x.unsqueeze(1)  -- B,H,L,C  (expensive: B*H*L*L*C)\n# But we don'\"'t need the full u1, only its norm and... no, we also need m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0)\n# And we need y = (r*m) @ wv\"'^T\n# So y = r * (p_mix @ x) @ wv''^T = r * p_mix @ (x @ wv''^T) = r * (p_mix @ v)\n# That'\"'s exactly what v-space does\"'! p_mix @ v then multiply by r.\n# Only the norm needs the C-space computation.\n#\n# So Gram is the right approach for the norm. Let me optimize the Gram path.\n#\n# Key idea: We have p_mix = (1-b)*p0 + b*p1\n# We need: norm1 = ||p1 @ x|| = sqrt((p1 @ G) * p1 sum)\n#          normm = ||p_mix @ x|| = sqrt((p_mix @ G) * p_mix sum)\n# \n# Method 1 (current): compute p1@G and p_mix@G separately (2 matmuls)\n# Method 2: compute p0@G and p1@G, then normm from norm0, norm1, cross (2 matmuls + some extra ops)\n# Method 3: compute p0@G once, then... hmm\n#\n# Actually: both methods need 2 matmuls. The question is which 2 and how much extra work.\n# Let me profile the total end-to-end for the non-alpha=1 case.\n\n# Another idea: can we save the v-projection entirely by working in x-space and\n# using a single matmul at the end? Let'\"'s compare:\n# V-space approach: 3 projections (q,k,v) + p@v (small) + gram norm (2 matmuls)\n# X-space approach: 2 projections (q,k) + p@x (large, 1 matmul) + final wv projection (1 matmul)\n# \n# For L=256, C=1280, H=20, D=64, B=1:\n# Projections: \n#   v-space: 3 * (B*L*C*H*D) = 3 * 1*256*1280*20*64 = 1.26B\n#   x-space: 2 * (B*L*C*H*D) + (B*H*L*C * H*D) ... wait that's wrong\n#   x-space p@x: B*H*L*L*C = 1*20*256*256*1280 = 1.68B\n#   x-space final y = (r*m) @ wv\"'^T: shape (B,H,L,C) @ (H,D,C)''^T -> ... \n#   Actually: y[b,h,l,d] = sum_c (r*u)[b,h,l,c] * wv[h,d,c]\n#   If we reshape: (B*H*L, C) @ (H*D, C)''^T? No, wv is per-head.\n#   Could do einsum or per-head.\n# \n# Total FLOPs:\n# V-space: 3*B*L*H*D*C + 2*B*H*L*L*D (logits+softmax) + 2*B*H*L*L*L (gram) + B*H*L*L*D (p@v)\n# = 3*BLHDC + 2BHL''^2D + 2BHL''^3 + BHL''^2D = 3*BLHDC + 3*BHL''^2D + 2*BHL''^3\n#\n# X-space: 2*B*L*H*D*C + 2*B*H*L*L*D (logits) + B*H*L*L*C (p_mix@x for output) + 2*B*H*L*L*L (gram norms) + B*H*L*C*D (final wv)\n# Wait, we still need gram for norms. Or we could compute norm directly from p@x result.\n# Actually in x-space, we compute p_mix@x anyway, so norm is basically free (just a norm op)!\n# And p1_norm: we'\"'d need p1@x separately... \n#\n# X-space revised:\n#   2 projections (q,k): 2*B*L*H*D*C\n#   logits + 2 softmaxes\n#   p0@x: B*H*L*L*C (for u0 = P0 x)\n#   p1@x: B*H*L*L*C (for u1 = P1 x, also gives norm for free)\n#   m = u0 + beta*(u1-u0) (elementwise)\n#   norm_m from m (free)\n#   ratio computation\n#   final: (r*m) projected through wv: B*H*L*C*D (per head)\n#\n# Total FLOPs for x-space: 2*BLHDC + 2*BHL\"'^2D + 2*BHL''^2C + BLHDC = 3*BLHDC + 2*BHL''^2D + 2*BHL''^2C\n# V-space total: 3*BLHDC + 3*BHL''^2D + 2*BHL''^3\n#\n# Difference x-space - v-space:\n#   = 2*BHL''^2C - 3*BHL''^2D - 2*BHL''^3\n#   = BHL''^2 * (2C - 3D - 2L)\n#\n# For L=256, C=1280, D=64: 2*1280 - 3*64 - 2*256 = 2560 - 192 - 512 = 1856 > 0\n# So v-space is cheaper!\n#\n# For L=64, C=512, D=64: 2*512 - 3*64 - 2*64 = 1024 - 192 - 128 = 704 > 0\n# Still v-space cheaper.\n#\n# v-space is always better when 2C > 3D + 2L, which is basically always true here.\n#\n# So v-space is the right approach. Let me focus on optimizing within v-space.\n#\n# Key cost breakdown (from profiling):\n# - Projections: ~45% (q, k, v)\n# - Gram norms: ~25-30%\n# - Softmax: ~10-20%\n# - Logits + p@v + p_mix: ~10%\n#\n# Projections are hard to optimize further (they'\"'re already 2D matmuls).\n# Gram norms: we can use G_full (pre-expanded) for ~15% speedup on the matmul.\n# Softmax: already optimized with view trick.\n#\n# Let me try one more thing: what if we combine p0 softmax with logits computation\n# by doing everything in the reshaped 2D space?\n\nprint('Key insight: pre-expanding G gives ~15% speedup on gram matmuls')\nprint('That should give ~3-4% overall improvement')\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
