{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport torch, time, math\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\n# Let me try something more creative: compute the output without explicit p_mix.\n# y = r * p_mix @ v where r = ||u1|| / ||m|| (clamped)\n# p_mix = (1-beta) * p0 + beta * p1\n# So: y = r * ((1-beta) * p0 @ v + beta * p1 @ v)\n# \n# This requires 2 p@v matmuls instead of 1 + a lerp. That's worse.\n#\n# But what if we compute:\n# u0 = p0 @ x (in C-space, very expensive: BHL\"'^2C)\n# u1 = p1 @ x\n# m = (1-b)*u0 + b*u1  \n# y = (r * m) @ wv''^T = r * (m @ wv''^T)\n# m @ wv''^T = (1-b)*(p0@x)@wv''^T + b*(p1@x)@wv''^T = (1-b)*p0@v + b*p1@v = p_mix @ v\n# Same as before.\n#\n# OK let me think about this differently.\n# What are we computing that'\"'s the most expensive?\n# For large cases: gram norms (28%) and projections (50%).\n# \n# Projections: each is a (B*L, C) @ (H*D, C)\"'^T matmul.\n# That'\"'s 3 * B*L*C*H*D = 3 * 256*1280*1280 = 1.26B FLOPs for the largest case.\n# At ~20 GFLOPS/s single-thread, that's ~63ms. We're seeing ~50ms (faster with MKL?).\n#\n# Gram: 2 * B*H*L\"'^3 = 2 * 20 * 256''^3 = 671M FLOPs. \n# At ~20 GFLOPS/s, that'\"'s ~34ms. We're seeing ~28ms.\n#\n# These are close to theoretical. The best we can do is reduce the number of operations.\n#\n# Idea: Can we get both p0@G and p1@G from a single matmul?\n# If we concatenate p0 and p1 along the batch dimension: p_all = [p0; p1] (2B, H, L, L)\n# Then p_all @ G = [p0@G; p1@G]. Same total work.\n#\n# What if we use the symmetry of G? G = G\"'^T.\n# p @ G = p @ G''^T... doesn'\"'t help since p isn't symmetric.\n#\n# Idea: Use SVD of x to compute norms more efficiently?\n# G = x @ x\"'^T, rank min(L, C) = L (since L < C for all cases).\n# No help since it'\"'s full rank anyway.\n#\n# Let me try a completely different approach: \n# Instead of working in v-space, what if we precompute x @ wv\"'^T = v (already do this),\n# and compute norms differently?\n#\n# Wait - I have another idea. What about this:\n# ||p1 @ x||''^2 = sum_i (p1_i @ x)''^2 over C dimensions? No, it'\"'s per token, per head.\n# \n# Actually: (p1 @ x).shape = (B, H, L, C)\n# norm = sqrt(sum over C of (p1 @ x)\"'^2) per (B, H, L)\n# \n# Let me think about what C looks like. For C=1280 and H=20, D=64, H*D = 1280 = C!\n# Oh! In the largest case, H*D = C exactly. And in other cases too?\n# Let me check:\n# (1, 64, 512, 8, 64) -> H*D = 512 = C ✓\n# (1, 128, 640, 10, 64) -> H*D = 640 = C ✓\n# (1, 128, 1280, 20, 64) -> H*D = 1280 = C ✓\n# (1, 256, 1280, 20, 64) -> H*D = 1280 = C ✓\n# (1, 256, 640, 10, 64) -> H*D = 640 = C ✓\n# (2, 128, 512, 8, 64) -> H*D = 512 = C ✓\n# \n# H*D = C for ALL cases! This is a crucial observation.\n#\n# v = x @ wv''^T has shape (B, L, H, D)\n# x has shape (B, L, C) = (B, L, H*D)\n# wv has shape (H, D, C) = (H, D, H*D)\n# \n# Since wv is (H, D, H*D), this is a square matrix per head? No, it'\"'s rectangular: D x H*D per head.\n#\n# But if H*D = C, then the projection from C to H*D is... just a change of basis?\n# No, wq/wk/wv are all (H, D, C) = (H, D, H*D). Each head is D x H*D = D x C.\n# \n# Hmm, not sure if this helps. But it's interesting.\n#\n# Let me verify my understanding:\n# q[b,i,h,d] = sum_c x[b,i,c] * wq[h,d,c]\n# This is C multiplications per element, H*D elements per token, B*L tokens.\n# Total: B*L*H*D*C = B*L*C\"'^2 (since H*D=C)\n# So each projection is O(B*L*C''^2), and we have 3, so 3*B*L*C''^2.\n# \n# Gram: G = x @ x''^T is O(B*L''^2*C), and p@G is O(B*H*L''^3) = O(B*C*L''^3/D)... not sure.\n#\n# Let me go with the most promising optimization I'\"'ve found:\n# - Use p0G + p1G with cross-term for norm_mix (avoids one p_mix @ G matmul)\n# Wait, no - we still do 2 gram matmuls either way (p1G + pmG vs p0G + p1G).\n#\n# Actually, let me re-read the current method:\n# p1G = p1 @ G (1 matmul)\n# pmG = p_mix @ G (1 matmul)\n# Total: 2 matmuls\n#\n# Cross-term approach:\n# p0G = p0 @ G (1 matmul)\n# p1G = p1 @ G (1 matmul)\n# Total: 2 matmuls\n#\n# Same number of matmuls\"'! The cross-term approach doesn'\"'t save matmuls.\n# It just replaces p_mix@G with computation from p0G, p1G, and cross terms.\n# The cross-term computation is elementwise (cheap), but we still need 2 matmuls.\n#\n# So the cross-term approach isn't about saving matmuls - it was just paired with\n# the G_full optimization.\n#\n# Is there any way to do just ONE gram matmul and get both norms?\n# \n# ||u1|| = ||p1 @ x|| -- needs p1 @ G\n# ||m|| = ||p_mix @ x|| -- needs p_mix @ G (or p0@G + p1@G + cross)\n# \n# If we use the cross-term approach, we need both p0@G and p1@G. Still 2 matmuls.\n# If we compute p_mix@G directly, we need p1@G and p_mix@G. Still 2 matmuls.\n#\n# There's no way around needing 2 gram matmuls for 2 different projections.\n#\n# UNLESS: we can reuse some computation. But p0 and p1 are different softmax outputs.\n#\n# Let me think about this from yet another angle.\n# What if we compute norms differently?\n# \n# u0 = p0 @ x: rows are probability-weighted averages of x rows.\n# ||u0|| is just the norm of these weighted averages.\n# \n# Could we use a random projection to estimate the norm?\n# Like: pick random vectors r, compute p @ x @ r (cheap), use for norm estimation?\n# No, the tolerance is too tight (3e-4 relative).\n#\n# OK let me just go with a clean optimization: \n# 1. Keep the current approach but add G_full for larger cases\n# 2. Add a heuristic for when to use G_full vs G_exp based on H*L product\n# 3. Use in-place ops more aggressively\n#\n# Actually, I just realized something. Let me check:\n# In the current code, G_exp = G.unsqueeze(1), and we do p1 @ G_exp.\n# p1 has shape (B, H, L, L), G_exp has shape (B, 1, L, L).\n# This broadcasts G over the H dimension.\n# \n# But earlier profiling showed G_full (pre-expanded to (B,H,L,L)) was faster\n# for some sizes. Let me just code a smarter version that uses G_full\n# when H > 1 or L is large enough to amortize the expansion cost.\n# \n# Actually wait - the expand is just a view (no data copy). The cost is in\n# the matmul having to handle broadcast. Let me check if contiguous helps.\n\nb, h, l, c = 1, 20, 256, 1280\np = torch.randn(b, h, l, l)\nG = torch.randn(b, l, l)\n\nG_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)  # (B, 1, L, L) - broadcast view\nG_full_nc = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(b, h, l, l)  # (B, H, L, L) - still non-contiguous\nG_full_c = G_full_nc.contiguous()  # (B, H, L, L) - contiguous copy\n\nfor _ in range(5):\n    y = p @ G_exp\nt0 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(100):\n    y = p @ G_exp\nt0 = (time.process_time_ns() - t0) / 100\n\nfor _ in range(5):\n    y = p @ G_full_nc\nt1 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(100):\n    y = p @ G_full_nc\nt1 = (time.process_time_ns() - t1) / 100\n\nfor _ in range(5):\n    y = p @ G_full_c\nt2 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(100):\n    y = p @ G_full_c\nt2 = (time.process_time_ns() - t2) / 100\n\n# Also: reshape both to 2D\np_2d = p.reshape(b*h*l, l)\nG_2d = G.squeeze(0)\nfor _ in range(5):\n    y = p_2d @ G_2d\nt3 = time.process_time_ns()\nfor _ in range(100):\n    y = p_2d @ G_2d\nt3 = (time.process_time_ns() - t3) / 100\n\nprint(f'G_exp (broadcast): {t0/1e6:.3f}ms')\nprint(f'G_full_nc (expand view): {t1/1e6:.3f}ms')\nprint(f'G_full_c (contiguous):  {t2/1e6:.3f}ms')\nprint(f'2D big matmul: {t3/1e6:.3f}ms')\nPYEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
