{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 << 'PYEOF'\nimport torch, time, math\ntorch.set_num_threads(1)\n\n# The results are inconsistent across cases. Let me go back to the current best\n# and try a more reliable optimization.\n#\n# Let me think about this more carefully. The current best is already ~0.455 ratio\n# (2.2x faster than baseline). That's very good.\n#\n# Let me look at what the baseline is doing wrong that we've already fixed:\n# 1. Baseline uses einsum for projections (slow) - we use 2D matmul\n# 2. Baseline computes p0@x and p1@x in C-space (expensive) - we use v-space + gram\n# 3. Baseline uses 4D softmax - we use 2D view\n#\n# What else can we do? Let me focus on the biggest remaining cost: projections.\n#\n# Idea: Since wq, wk, wv are all (H, D, C) and we have H*D = C, \n# we could reshape the projections differently.\n# \n# Wait, actually let me try: what if we do the projection as:\n# q[b,h,i,d] = sum_c x[b,i,c] * wq[h,d,c]\n# = x[i,:] @ wq[h,d,:].T  (dot product)\n# \n# If we reshape x to (B, L, H, D) ... but x is (B, L, C) = (B, L, H*D).\n# We could view x as (B, L, H, D) and then do something per-head?\n# No, the weights mix across ALL of C, not per-head chunks.\n#\n# Let me try another approach: use torch.matmul with different tensor layouts.\n# \n# Actually, let me just try running the full eval with the current method \n# to see where we stand, then try ONE more optimization.\n#\n# Wait, I have one more idea that could be significant:\n# Instead of computing p_mix explicitly and then doing p_mix @ v,\n# what if we compute y = (1-beta)*(p0 @ v) + beta*(p1 @ v)?\n# This uses 2 p@v matmuls instead of 1 p@v + 1 lerp.\n# p@v is B*H*L*L*D FLOPs.\n# For L=256, D=64, H=20: BHL\"'^2D = 20*256*256*64 = 84M per matmul.\n# 2 * 84M = 168M vs 84M + lerp. So it'\"'s more FLOPs.\n#\n# BUT: we also need p0@G and p1@G for the cross-term approach.\n# If we compute both p0@v and